@notice Default reporter cut (basis points) of an equivocation stake seizure, used
         when the `_slashReporterRewardBps` slot is unset (0). 3000 = 30% reporter,
         70% burned.

@notice Default ceiling on the exclusion backoff, in SELECTION epochs. The ladder is
         LINEAR in the episode count (1, 2, 3, …) — a geometric tail was declined — so
         this caps the ladder rather than shaping it.

@notice The LATEST SCHEDULED committee-size cap — what governance last set, which
         during the epoch of a change is NOT yet the cap selection cuts with.
 @dev    Kept O(1) and behaviorally identical for its heuristic readers. Any consumer
         that must be epoch-correct — above all the selection view — MUST use
         `getActiveValidatorsLengthAt`.

@notice The committee-size cap in force for `epoch`.
 @dev    Immutable for every epoch that has already started: the setter only ever
         schedules `fromEpoch = currentEpoch + 1`, so no call can change the answer
         for `epoch <= currentEpoch`. This is the leg that makes
         `StakingLayout.getValidatorsAt` a pure function of its epoch argument.
         Backward scan; governance writes are rare, so it is O(1-2) in practice.

@dev Non-zero only: once a damage-coverage fund is wired, governance rotates it to another
      fund rather than back to burn (the dead-address burn is the genesis default, reachable
      only while the slot is still unset). Mirrors the setBlsVerifier / setEvidenceDecoder idiom.

@dev Ceiling on the LINEAR exclusion ladder, in selection epochs. There is deliberately
      no companion "base" knob: the duration is `min(kickCount, cap)` and reads no base,
      so shipping one would be a live governance control that changes nothing.

@dev RAW getter: true == the production-liveness tier is OFF. Seeded TRUE at init (the
      tier ships flag-off), which is the OPPOSITE default to
      `getParticipationJailDisabled` — do not pattern-match the two.

@dev F1 floor check shared by both setters and `initialize`.
 @dev F1 floor check. Widens to uint256 internally so callers never have to
      remember the cast (a missing one would compute the window in checked
      uint32 and revert with a spurious overflow on a large-but-valid config).

